    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Carbon cycle-climate modeling",
    "problem": "Existing long-term studies, often using simpler models, have not adequately quantified the regional-scale reversibility of greenhouse gas impacts, as these models cannot represent the internal climate variability needed to distinguish forced anthropogenic changes from natural fluctuations.",
    "method": "This study quantifies the reversibility of greenhouse gas impacts by running multi-century emission commitment scenarios with a coupled carbon cycle-climate model and comparing the magnitude of forced regional changes against the model's internal, unforced climate variability.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify the reversibility and irreversibility of long-term impacts from 21st-century greenhouse gas emissions by comparing forced regional changes in climate and ocean acidification with internal climate variability using a coupled carbon cycle-climate model.",
    "model": [
        "NCAR CSM1.4-carbon"
    ],
